CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ three levels: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ two levels: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [temp.spec.partial.match]/1, [class.static.data]/4, [temp.inst]/16:
F<W<W<W<int>>>> matches the partial specialization F<W<T>>, whose constant
member is F<T>::value + 1, so its value is 3.  The front end skipped any
third nested instantiation of the same template with type arguments (a
recursion guard), so F<W<int>> was never instantiated, the inner
initializers stayed unresolved, and both assertions failed silently.  A
type argument that is a proper part of the enclosing instances' arguments
converges and is now instantiated.  test_enumerator.desc (an enumerator)
and test_base_class.desc (the value inherited from a base class) were
conversion errors ("instantiating 'F' with <struct nil>").  The base-class
form is libc++'s __find_max_align, the default argument of
std::aligned_storage: `aligned_storage<24>::type' failed, so std::function's
__value_func::__buf_ was missing and libc++'s ~__value_func
(function.h:394) was dropped.  bin53 fails all three.  g++ 13 and clang++ 18
accept these programs, and they fail only their WRONG assertion.
